{"schemaVersion":1,"storyId":"two-ways-to-say-thank-you","version":1,"title":"Two Ways to Say Thank You","ageTiers":["5-6","7-8"],"language":"en","narrationLocale":"en-GH","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Akua’s school in Accra had children from many families. At home, Akua spoke Twi. Her new friend Sefakor spoke Ewe.","sentences":["Akua’s school in Accra had children from many families.","At home, Akua spoke Twi.","Her new friend Sefakor spoke Ewe."],"image":"sha256:ec1b84a0252b92234b91b7429dba289a678412254dc8194ee53037c6cd3bbac7","alt":"Children in school uniforms chat in a sunny schoolyard.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:09dd9974e634ee34e92512a570df60c0e6c79375bb28f4f73771c2caa2a47de1","durationMs":11040,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,5027],[5027,8080],[8080,11040]],"words":[[0,449],[449,988],[988,1168],[1168,1617],[1617,1886],[1886,2604],[2604,2963],[2963,3322],[3322,5027],[5027,5207],[5207,6015],[6015,6374],[6374,6823],[6823,8080],[8080,8349],[8349,8618],[8618,9157],[9157,9785],[9785,10234],[10234,11040]]}}},{"n":2,"text":"On Monday, Sefakor lent Akua a blue pencil. ‘Medaase,’ said Akua. That means thank you in Twi. Sefakor smiled.","sentences":["On Monday, Sefakor lent Akua a blue pencil.","‘Medaase,’ said Akua.","That means thank you in Twi.","Sefakor smiled."],"image":"sha256:b5df5ec5309559042622caab76288c75b1a200ccb4f07d34b2d6c6f8e3561dde","alt":"One girl lends a blue pencil to another, who smiles in thanks.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:cb663d276566e41ce819081d5e9e7f53f83d76082ece86aeb1343ed3dfc7ce9a","durationMs":12360,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,4704],[4704,7564],[7564,10607],[10607,12360]],"words":[[0,184],[184,1199],[1199,1845],[1845,2214],[2214,2583],[2583,2767],[2767,3136],[3136,4704],[4704,5811],[5811,6180],[6180,7564],[7564,7933],[7933,8394],[8394,8855],[8855,9132],[9132,9316],[9316,10607],[10607,11253],[11253,12360]]}}},{"n":3,"text":"On Tuesday, Akua shared her bread with Sefakor. ‘Akpé,’ said Sefakor. That means thank you in Ewe. Akua said it back: ‘Akpé!’","sentences":["On Tuesday, Akua shared her bread with Sefakor.","‘Akpé,’ said Sefakor.","That means thank you in Ewe.","Akua said it back: ‘Akpé!’"],"image":"sha256:c3c78ce12d4b2f08eb304dee17deef1f3176f9c9a7cacc217f6b441477bdaefd","alt":"The girls share a piece of bread under a tree.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:73ad045bd7f65a3f5bd703e72cb3674a751d680875a988bed2938df336193698","durationMs":12440,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,4633],[4633,7206],[7206,10037],[10037,12440]],"words":[[0,172],[172,1202],[1202,1545],[1545,2060],[2060,2317],[2317,2746],[2746,3089],[3089,4633],[4633,5319],[5319,5662],[5662,7206],[7206,7549],[7549,7978],[7978,8407],[8407,8664],[8664,8836],[8836,10037],[10037,10380],[10380,10723],[10723,10895],[10895,11667],[11667,12440]]}}},{"n":4,"text":"Their classmate Alhassan listened. ‘In Hausa we say Nagode,’ he said. Soon all three friends were saying thank you in three languages.","sentences":["Their classmate Alhassan listened.","‘In Hausa we say Nagode,’ he said.","Soon all three friends were saying thank you in three languages."],"image":"sha256:71f1ddbbe50414f98e207453bfec67bd60b14bc661b40b59c9a34ec115cad325","alt":"Three children laugh together, each speaking in turn.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:7f4e1d4325a42d78371fdfd7c732aa3f59257646f008a4a8b70935c352c36712","durationMs":12480,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,3655],[3655,7220],[7220,12480]],"words":[[0,446],[446,1248],[1248,1961],[1961,3655],[3655,3833],[3833,4279],[4279,4457],[4457,4724],[4724,5705],[5705,5883],[5883,7220],[7220,7577],[7577,7844],[7844,8290],[8290,8914],[8914,9271],[9271,9806],[9806,10252],[10252,10519],[10519,10697],[10697,11143],[11143,12480]]}}},{"n":5,"text":"Akua had an idea. She asked her teacher, Madam Serwaa, ‘Can we make a thank-you wall?’ Madam Serwaa smiled. ‘What a good idea!’","sentences":["Akua had an idea.","She asked her teacher, Madam Serwaa, ‘Can we make a thank-you wall?’ Madam Serwaa smiled.","‘What a good idea!’"],"image":"sha256:90da5be77b3e21d2ff94c509672e80eeea94c2dd71de99fed410dd25f71343a0","alt":"The girl speaks eagerly to a smiling teacher beside a bare classroom wall.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:2dfee19fb1f0b610477bdbf1c722a0667cb8605b8abce982e7b898b0753eb03b","durationMs":12920,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,2138],[2138,11138],[11138,12920]],"words":[[0,356],[356,623],[623,801],[801,2138],[2138,2405],[2405,2851],[2851,3118],[3118,4187],[4187,4633],[4633,5613],[5613,5880],[5880,6058],[6058,6414],[6414,6592],[6592,7305],[7305,8642],[8642,9088],[9088,9623],[9623,11138],[11138,11494],[11494,11672],[11672,12028],[12028,12920]]}}},{"n":6,"text":"The class wrote thank you in every language they knew on coloured paper. There was English, Twi, Ewe, Hausa and more.","sentences":["The class wrote thank you in every language they knew on coloured paper.","There was English, Twi, Ewe, Hausa and more."],"image":"sha256:d9ad2b28fb430542387e3c50815d5b19e2bae92430929a65b20a6e2016a43fd7","alt":"Children cut and write on coloured paper at their desks.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:ba4c590374801fadb4ad7530bad87afa7992b862dee216ca4eef0899100d2ede","durationMs":10880,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,6142],[6142,10880]],"words":[[0,263],[263,702],[702,1141],[1141,1580],[1580,1843],[1843,2018],[2018,2457],[2457,3159],[3159,3510],[3510,3861],[3861,4036],[4036,4738],[4738,6142],[6142,6581],[6581,6844],[6844,7897],[7897,8599],[8599,9301],[9301,9740],[9740,10003],[10003,10880]]}}},{"n":7,"text":"Each child asked someone at home to teach them a new word. The wall grew and grew until it covered the whole door.","sentences":["Each child asked someone at home to teach them a new word.","The wall grew and grew until it covered the whole door."],"image":"sha256:e207cb89d40aa1f8b1092e03dcaf0db5a6e75e1649deb79dab4db8892c85df2e","alt":"A wall covered in colourful paper words and a door nearly hidden by them.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:d41ab77e4a93aeecc17d8da6e45249555b5cf42c0e39da94ef3ba62e38228081","durationMs":9400,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,5047],[5047,9400]],"words":[[0,348],[348,783],[783,1218],[1218,1827],[1827,2001],[2001,2349],[2349,2523],[2523,2958],[2958,3306],[3306,3480],[3480,3741],[3741,5047],[5047,5308],[5308,5656],[5656,6004],[6004,6265],[6265,6613],[6613,7048],[7048,7222],[7222,7831],[7831,8092],[8092,8527],[8527,9400]]}}},{"n":8,"text":"Akua loved reading the wall. ‘Different words,’ she said, ‘but the same kind feeling!’ The end.","sentences":["Akua loved reading the wall.","‘Different words,’ she said, ‘but the same kind feeling!’ The end."],"image":"sha256:3aa1898f6a5fd471f0bed07a5e9e73c845ead8e8923634c42395b9a97c19c7ee","alt":"The girl stands smiling in front of the full, colourful thank-you wall.","audio":{"hash":"sha256:0bdd0660a56f7a4cb92ddb394c991a2915f8882f5afc3866f60b3ab70365a337","durationMs":9680,"timing":{"granularity":"word","method":"tts-marks","verified":true,"sentences":[[0,3019],[3019,9680]],"words":[[0,355],[355,799],[799,1421],[1421,1687],[1687,3019],[3019,3818],[3818,4706],[4706,4972],[4972,5771],[5771,6037],[6037,6303],[6303,6658],[6658,7013],[7013,8612],[8612,8878],[8878,9680]]}}}],"activities":[{"id":"thanks-c1","type":"comprehension","afterPage":3,"question":"Which language is Akpé from?","options":["Ewe","Twi","Hausa"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"thanks-voc","type":"vocabulary","afterPage":4,"word":"classmate","definition":"A child who is in the same class as you.","distractors":["A teacher","A school bag"]},{"id":"thanks-ph","type":"phonics","afterPage":4,"targetSound":"th","prompt":"Which words start with the th sound, like thank?","options":["thank","three","wall","door"],"correctIndexes":[0,1]},{"id":"thanks-p1","type":"prediction","afterPage":5,"prompt":"The class is making a thank-you wall. How do you think it will look at the end?"},{"id":"thanks-s1","type":"sequencing","afterPage":8,"prompt":"Put the story in order.","items":["Sefakor lent Akua a pencil.","Alhassan shared the Hausa word.","The class made a thank-you wall."]},{"id":"thanks-sel","type":"sel","afterPage":8,"prompt":"How do you feel when someone says thank you in a language you know from home? What word would you add to the wall?","feelingOptions":["Proud","Welcome","Curious","Shy"]}],"glossary":[{"word":"lent","definition":"Let someone use something for a while.","pages":[2]},{"word":"classmate","definition":"A child who is in the same class as you.","pages":[4]},{"word":"languages","definition":"Ways that people speak and write to each other.","pages":[4]}],"assets":{"sha256:09dd9974e634ee34e92512a570df60c0e6c79375bb28f4f73771c2caa2a47de1":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/audio/09dd9974e634.mp3","bytes":44589,"type":"audio/mpeg"},"sha256:ec1b84a0252b92234b91b7429dba289a678412254dc8194ee53037c6cd3bbac7":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/candidates/two-ways-to-say-thank-you/page1-1.webp","bytes":85584,"type":"image/webp"},"sha256:cb663d276566e41ce819081d5e9e7f53f83d76082ece86aeb1343ed3dfc7ce9a":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/audio/cb663d276566.mp3","bytes":49869,"type":"audio/mpeg"},"sha256:b5df5ec5309559042622caab76288c75b1a200ccb4f07d34b2d6c6f8e3561dde":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/candidates/two-ways-to-say-thank-you/page2-1.webp","bytes":70416,"type":"image/webp"},"sha256:73ad045bd7f65a3f5bd703e72cb3674a751d680875a988bed2938df336193698":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/audio/73ad045bd7f6.mp3","bytes":50253,"type":"audio/mpeg"},"sha256:c3c78ce12d4b2f08eb304dee17deef1f3176f9c9a7cacc217f6b441477bdaefd":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/candidates/two-ways-to-say-thank-you/page3-1.webp","bytes":85146,"type":"image/webp"},"sha256:7f4e1d4325a42d78371fdfd7c732aa3f59257646f008a4a8b70935c352c36712":{"path":"/story-spark-preview/audi